"""Chapter 2 (literature survey) and Chapter 3 (problem statement)."""

BLOCKS = [
    ("h1", "CHAPTER – 2: LITERATURE SURVEY"),
    ("p", "Dhartisense draws on six research areas that are usually studied separately: crop suitability, irrigation scheduling, nutrient advice, photo-based disease recognition, satellite vegetation monitoring and language-model assistants. This chapter reviews each one, giving most space to the work the project uses directly, and closes with the gap that remains."),
    ("h2", "2.1 Machine learning and sensing in agriculture"),
    ("p", "Reviews of machine learning in farming agree on where the effort goes. Liakos et al. sorted the literature into crop, livestock, water and soil management [@liakos]. Benos et al. repeated the exercise for papers from 2018 to 2020 and found crop management still the largest group, with satellites and unmanned vehicles supplying much of the sensor data [@benos]. Kamilaris and Prenafeta-Boldú surveyed deep learning in agriculture and found that most of the successful work concerned image recognition, the task for which labelled data is easiest to collect [@kamilaris]."),
    ("p", "Systems built on sensors and machinery suit farms that can pay for equipment and keep records, which most smallholders cannot. A model is also only as good as its training data, and results move poorly between regions and seasons. Two choices in Dhartisense follow from this. The crop engine scores crops against published requirement ranges and checks the result on real district statistics, instead of fitting a classifier to a small table of soil and weather values. The plant model is evaluated on field photographs, where laboratory images alone would flatter it."),
    ("h2", "2.2 Crop suitability assessment"),
    ("p", "The FAO EcoCrop database records, for each crop, the absolute and optimal ranges of temperature, rainfall and soil pH, the preferred soil texture and the length of the growing cycle [@ecocrop]. Each range has four numbers: the value below which the crop fails, the lower and upper ends of the optimal band, and the value above which it fails again. A four-point range converts directly into a trapezoid. The score is zero below the absolute minimum, climbs to one at the start of the optimal band, stays there, and falls back to zero at the absolute maximum. Nothing has to be trained, so there is no risk of fitting noise in a small data set."),
    ("p", "Egbebiyi et al. used Ecocrop, driven by regional climate-model output, to estimate how suitability for six West African crops changes at 1.5, 2 and 3 °C of global warming [@egbebiyi]. That study applies the requirement ranges to projected weather over a region. Dhartisense applies them to the recorded weather and the soil of a single field."),
    ("p", "A requirement database says what a crop can tolerate. It says nothing about what farmers in a region choose to grow, and those choices carry information about markets, labour, tradition and risk that no weather table holds. The Government of India publishes district-wise area, production and yield for each crop and season [@districts]. Dhartisense uses these figures as a second signal and combines the two in Chapter 4, so that a crop ranks high only if it fits the field and is also grown nearby."),
    ("h2", "2.3 Irrigation scheduling and nutrient management"),
    ("p", "FAO Irrigation and Drainage Paper 56 remains the standard reference for crop water requirements [@fao56]. It computes reference evapotranspiration ET₀ from weather, multiplies it by a crop coefficient K_{c} that changes with growth stage to get crop evapotranspiration, and keeps a daily account of the water in the root zone. When the water used since the last refill passes the readily available water, the crop is stressed and irrigation is due. The paper tabulates K_{c}, stage lengths, rooting depths and depletion fractions for most field crops, and the FAO Irrigation Manual gives typical application efficiencies for drip, sprinkler and surface irrigation [@savva]."),
    ("p", "Pereira et al. reviewed soil water balance models built on the FAO-56 method and its dual-coefficient form, and listed the links to sensors, cloud services and farm practice as open problems for real-time scheduling [@pereira20]. The same group later revised the mid-season and end-season coefficients for field crops [@pereira21]. Dhartisense keeps its coefficients in stage tables, so a revised set can replace them without touching the calculation. Saggi and Jain surveyed decision-support systems that use machine learning to schedule irrigation [@saggi]. Dhartisense takes the water-balance route instead, because it has no sensor records to learn from. Open-Meteo serves daily FAO ET₀ and rain for any coordinates, past and forecast [@openmeteo], so the inputs arrive automatically and the account can be run for one field at a time."),
    ("p", "For nutrients, the Indian Soil Health Card scheme rates a soil sample as low, medium or high for available nitrogen, phosphorus and potassium and issues crop-wise recommendations [@shc]. State agricultural universities publish general crop doses, for example the Tamil Nadu Agricultural University crop production guide [@tnau]. A common practice is to raise the general dose when the soil test is low and cut it when the test is high. Dhartisense applies that practice to numbers the farmer types in, so it needs no sensor and no laboratory integration."),
    ("h2", "2.4 Image-based plant disease recognition"),
    ("p", "Ferentinos compared several deep architectures on an image database of more than 80,000 leaf photographs and reported accuracy above 99% [@ferentinos]. Barbedo asked why such systems struggle outside the laboratory and identified nine factors, among them busy backgrounds that contain shapes resembling symptoms [@barbedo]. Later reviews describe the same gap between laboratory and field results: images with clutter, shadow and mixed symptoms remain hard, and a classifier scored only on clean images overstates what it can do in a field [@lu2021; @dhaka]."),
    ("p", "PlantDoc was built to narrow this gap. Its photographs come from the web and are annotated for visual disease detection, so they show real backgrounds and uneven quality [@plantdoc]. The collection holds fewer than 3,000 images, which limits how precisely a model can be evaluated on it. Dhartisense trains on PlantDoc and PlantVillage together and reports results on the PlantDoc test split only, because that split is the closer match to a farmer's photo."),
    ("p", "The backbone is ConvNeXt-Tiny, a convolutional network updated with design choices borrowed from vision transformers. It has about 28 million parameters and competes with transformers of similar size on ImageNet while keeping a convolutional structure [@convnext]. Starting from ImageNet weights helps most when the target data set is small, as it is here."),
    ("p", "A classifier that is right 80% of the time is useful only if it knows when it is wrong. Minderer et al. compared the calibration of recent image classifiers and found that newer architectures, notably those without convolutions, are often better calibrated, and that architecture mattered more than model size or the amount of pretraining [@minderer]. A convolutional backbone therefore gives no guarantee of good calibration, and Dhartisense measures its own. Label smoothing, which the project uses in training, was reported to improve calibration as well as accuracy [@muller]. Gawlikowski et al. survey uncertainty estimation in deep networks, including test-time augmentation [@gawlikowski]. Dhartisense uses that idea by averaging the output for a photo and its mirror image. After training it scales the logits with one temperature and sets a confidence threshold so that accepted predictions reach a target accuracy."),
    ("h2", "2.5 Satellite vegetation monitoring"),
    ("p", "The normalised difference vegetation index compares reflectance in near-infrared and red light. Healthy leaves reflect strongly in the near-infrared and absorb red light, so green vegetation gives high values and bare soil gives low ones. Huang et al. review how the index is obtained and used, and list the problems that come with it, among them atmospheric effects, saturation and calibration [@huang]. The Sentinel-2 mission supplies 10 m bands in red and near-infrared with a revisit time of about five days when both satellites operate. Phiri et al. review its use for land cover and land use mapping and note that free access has encouraged its use in developing countries [@phiri]."),
    ("p", "Level-2A products include a scene classification layer that labels each pixel as vegetation, soil, water, cloud, cloud shadow, snow and so on. Microsoft's Planetary Computer exposes Sentinel-2 through the SpatioTemporal Asset Catalog interface and a statistics service that returns summary values for a polygon without downloading the image [@pc; @stac]. Dhartisense uses this to compute a field's mean NDVI and its histogram on the server, and uses the classification layer to ignore scenes where cloud or shadow covers the field itself."),
    ("h2", "2.6 Language models for agricultural advice"),
    ("p", "Zhu et al. review large language and vision-language models in agriculture and describe the field as new and still under-represented in the large-model literature [@zhu]. Tzachor et al. discuss the use of language models in agricultural extension, where they could widen access to advice in local languages, and set out the risks of a fluent answer that is wrong or unsafe [@tzachor]. Dhartisense takes that risk into account in a limited way. The assistant is told to use only the supplied field context, to avoid inventing measurements, diagnoses, pesticide rates or yield promises, and to ask one short question when information is missing [@groq]. Every numerical plan comes from a deterministic engine."),
    ("h2", "2.7 Summary and research gap"),
    ("p", "{tab:related} sets the approaches side by side. Each is well developed on its own. What the literature leaves open is their combination for a single field, for a user with no hardware and little time, with the uncertainty of each part made visible."),
    ("tab", "related", "Related approaches and how Dhartisense differs",
     ["Approach", "What it offers", "Limit for a smallholder", "Choice in Dhartisense"],
     [["Sensor-based precision agriculture [@benos]", "Variable-rate inputs from sensors and machinery", "Needs equipment and records", "Farmer entries and public data only"],
      ["Machine-learning crop recommenders [@liakos; @benos]", "A crop label from a table of soil and weather values", "Small tables of uncertain origin; one score for any field", "Published requirement ranges plus nearby practice, scored on held-out districts"],
      ["Plant disease CNNs [@ferentinos; @barbedo]", "High accuracy on laboratory images", "Accuracy falls on field photos; confidence not calibrated", "Lab and field training data, calibrated confidence, vision-model fallback"],
      ["FAO-56 scheduling [@fao56; @pereira20]", "A standard soil-water balance", "Manual inputs, written for agronomists", "Automatic weather, farmer observations override the model"],
      ["Satellite NDVI [@phiri; @pc]", "Free 10 m imagery every few days", "Needs GIS skills; clouds mislead", "Boundary in, cloud-screened reading and trend out"],
      ["Language-model advice [@tzachor; @zhu]", "Answers in local languages", "Fluent but possibly wrong", "Field context supplied; numbers come from engines; replies in five languages"]],
     [2150, 2450, 2650, 2917]),
    ("p", "Dhartisense does not claim a better crop-yield model, a better disease network or a better irrigation theory than any of these works. Its contribution is the assembly: one field profile feeding five engines, each built on an accepted method, evaluated where evaluation is possible, and presented with its limits."),

    ("h1", "CHAPTER – 3: PROBLEM STATEMENT"),
    ("h2", "3.1 Problem definition"),
    ("p", "Given the boundary of one field, a few facts the farmer can supply about its soil and water, and a planting date, the system should produce advice the farmer can act on in the next days and weeks. That means which crops fit, when and how much to irrigate, how much fertiliser to apply, how healthy the crop looks from space, and what a diseased leaf may be. The advice must be specific to the field, understandable without agronomic training, available in the farmer's language, and obtainable without paying for software or buying a device."),
    ("h2", "3.2 Gaps in current practice"),
    ("p", "Five gaps came up repeatedly when the project was scoped. They are listed here because each one maps to a requirement later in the chapter."),
    ("bul", ["Bulletins and calendars describe a district or a state. They ignore the weather history, the pH and the soil texture of the farmer's own field.",
             "One application shows weather, another identifies a leaf and a third gives a crop calendar. The farmer has to join the outputs and judge which to trust.",
             "Systems that schedule irrigation from soil-moisture probes or flow meters are out of reach for a farmer with only a phone.",
             "A single score or label with no reasons gives the farmer nothing to weigh against what they see in the field.",
             "A photo classifier that always names a disease, even for a poor photo or a crop it has not learned, can lead to the wrong treatment."]),
    ("h2", "3.3 Constraints"),
    ("p", "Several constraints shape the solution. The service must run on free tiers, so every external call has a quota to respect. The weather service is free for non-commercial use with fair-use limits, the language-model tier allows only a few thousand tokens a minute, the public place-search servers allow about one request a second for the whole application, and the satellite service is free for light use. The server therefore caches responses and limits each signed-in user's rate on the routes that spend a quota."),
    ("p", "Data licences limit what can be shipped. The crop requirement database and the district statistics are third-party data whose licence status is not fully settled, and PlantVillage images carry a share-alike licence that may extend to models trained on them. The repository therefore contains code only, and the data files are built locally from documented sources. Connectivity in rural areas is uneven, so pages must be light and no feature should depend on a build step or a large download. The farmer's data is also sensitive, since the exact location and boundary of a field and an email address are stored, so access must be limited to the owner."),
    ("h2", "3.4 Requirements"),
    ("p", "The requirements below were derived from the gaps and constraints. {tab:req} lists the functional ones, and the non-functional ones follow it."),
    ("tab", "req", "Functional requirements",
     ["ID", "Requirement", "Verified by"],
     [["F1", "A farmer can register, confirm the email, sign in, sign out and reset the password.", "Firebase flow, manual walk-through"],
      ["F2", "A farmer can create, edit and delete up to 50 fields, each with a boundary and soil and water details.", "Field store self-check"],
      ["F3", "The boundary can be traced by tapping corners or drawn freehand; area, perimeter, length, width and direction are computed.", "Manual use, screens in Chapter 5"],
      ["F4", "The five best crops for a field and planting date are shown with reasons and a match label.", "Benchmark, screens"],
      ["F5", "An irrigation schedule with dates and depths is produced for chosen crops, with calendar export.", "Self-check, worked example"],
      ["F6", "A fertiliser plan is produced from soil-test values for chosen crops.", "Self-check, worked example"],
      ["F7", "Satellite NDVI is shown as a map, a trend and plain notes for the field boundary.", "Self-check, screens"],
      ["F8", "A leaf photo yields a likely condition with confidence, or an honest statement of uncertainty.", "Test-set evaluation"],
      ["F9", "The farmer can ask questions in English, Hindi, Marathi, Bengali or Tamil and print a field report.", "Manual use, screens"]],
     [800, 6300, 3067], ["center", "left", "left"]),
    ("bul", ["Only the owner reads or writes a field, no secret reaches the browser, and abusive request rates are refused.",
             "Estimates, model outputs and farmer entries are labelled as such, and no output claims certainty.",
             "A missing data file or a failed external service degrades one feature instead of stopping the application.",
             "The interface works on phones from 320 px wide to large desktop screens, shows one result card at a time and uses plain words.",
             "The whole system can be hosted on free plans for a pilot."]),
]
